<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Buddhas of Bamiyan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Buddhas of Bamiyan were two monumental standing Buddha statues, 55 and 38 metres tall, carved into a cliff in the Bamiyan valley of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in the 6th century. Painted and plastered, they stood near a large Buddhist monastic complex on a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. The Taliban destroyed them with explosives in March 2001, an act condemned around the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buddhas-of-bamiyan.jpg" alt="Buddhas of Bamiyan" width="320" height="492" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Gordon · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buddha_of_Bamiyan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yet under their dominion Buddhism was predominant, with Zoroastrian and Manichaean minorities and many Hindu adherents; Balkh had some 100 monasteries and 30,000 monks, the capital of Chaghaniyan five, and the <b>Buddhas of Bamiyan</b> were begun under Hephthalite rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its monuments include the stupa of Sanchi, the painted caves of Ajanta, the colossal <b>Buddhas of Bamiyan</b>, destroyed by the Taliban in 2001, the temple-mountain of Borobudur, the pagodas of Bagan and the temples of Kyoto and Lhasa.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In March 2001 it destroyed the <b>Buddhas of Bamiyan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/islamic-emirate-of-afghanistan-1996-2001">Islamic Emirate of Afghanistan (1996-2001)</a></li></ul></section>
